WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.970
Start with Monday,
the ninth of January, twenty twelve.

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:09.090
At seven in the morning, we need a
forecast for the hour beginning at eight.

00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:13.470
The target hour is eight,
and the working day flag is one.

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:16.050
Those calendar facts are already known.

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:18.430
They form the pair eight, one.

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:22.110
The recorded outcome was four
hundred and twenty rentals.

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:26.010
We can inspect that answer now
because the hour is historical.

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:29.770
At the original issue time,
it was still in the future.

00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:33.390
Our predictor must select its
answer without that outcome.

00:00:33.740 --> 00:00:37.830
The outcome becomes useful later,
when we score the saved forecast.

00:00:40.292 --> 00:00:44.702
The existing constant predictor learned
one number from the training year.

00:00:45.312 --> 00:00:49.536
Eight thousand, six hundred and forty
five recorded hours contain one million,

00:00:49.536 --> 00:00:53.702
two hundred and forty three thousand,
one hundred and three rentals.

00:00:54.332 --> 00:00:58.209
Dividing the total by the count gives
about one hundred and forty three point

00:00:58.209 --> 00:01:00.382
seven nine rentals per hour.

00:01:01.152 --> 00:01:03.662
Every requested hour
receives that same value.

00:01:04.172 --> 00:01:05.862
This makes a useful reference,

00:01:06.052 --> 00:01:09.502
but it cannot distinguish a quiet
night from the morning commute.

00:01:10.092 --> 00:01:12.462
Missing source hours remain missing.

00:01:12.872 --> 00:01:17.372
We do not insert zero targets or change
the denominator to a complete calendar

00:01:17.372 --> 00:01:18.062
grid.

00:01:20.150 --> 00:01:24.060
Now select the training hours
whose key is eight, one.

00:01:24.470 --> 00:01:26.370
There are two hundred
and forty eight of them.

00:01:26.370 --> 00:01:30.319
Their observed training targets
total eighty six thousand,

00:01:30.319 --> 00:01:32.820
six hundred and seventy seven rentals.

00:01:33.470 --> 00:01:35.320
Divide that total by two
hundred and forty eight.

00:01:36.590 --> 00:01:41.150
The group mean is about three hundred and
forty nine point five zero rentals per

00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:41.740
hour.

00:01:42.370 --> 00:01:45.180
This is the number saved
for a working day at eight.

00:01:45.650 --> 00:01:50.900
Calling predict with the pair eight,
one looks up that saved value.

00:01:51.290 --> 00:01:55.770
We have changed the fitted function from
one global level to calendar dependent

00:01:55.770 --> 00:01:56.540
levels.

00:01:57.050 --> 00:02:00.240
No validation answer
participated in either fit.

00:02:02.608 --> 00:02:06.718
The working day group at two in the
morning has a different training mean.

00:02:07.108 --> 00:02:08.227
Its total is one thousand,

00:02:08.227 --> 00:02:12.978
seven hundred and ninety six across
two hundred and thirty nine hours.

00:02:13.428 --> 00:02:16.458
That gives seven point
five one rentals per hour.

00:02:16.948 --> 00:02:22.078
The real later outcome is five, so the
residual is positive two point five one.

00:02:22.708 --> 00:02:23.418
At eight,

00:02:23.468 --> 00:02:27.291
subtract four hundred and twenty from
three hundred and forty nine point five

00:02:27.291 --> 00:02:27.838
zero.

00:02:28.308 --> 00:02:31.338
The residual is negative
seventy point five zero,

00:02:31.708 --> 00:02:35.568
compared with negative two hundred and
seventy six point two one for the global

00:02:35.568 --> 00:02:36.278
reference.

00:02:37.048 --> 00:02:38.718
Both examples become closer.

00:02:39.348 --> 00:02:43.208
We still need the complete validation
comparison before making a broader

00:02:43.208 --> 00:02:44.398
improvement claim.

00:02:46.567 --> 00:02:49.357
Consider a different
valid calendar request,

00:02:49.847 --> 00:02:51.977
eight in the morning on a nonworking day.

00:02:52.507 --> 00:02:54.457
The key becomes eight, zero.

00:02:54.867 --> 00:02:58.487
Its training group has one hundred
and fifteen hours and eight thousand,

00:02:58.487 --> 00:03:00.537
seven hundred and ninety rentals.

00:03:01.227 --> 00:03:04.117
The saved mean is about
seventy six point four three.

00:03:04.707 --> 00:03:08.297
The hour stays eight,
but the selected parameter changes.

00:03:08.747 --> 00:03:13.537
This pairing can represent different daily
profiles for working and nonworking days.

00:03:14.027 --> 00:03:16.097
It does not fit a separate Monday effect.

00:03:16.727 --> 00:03:20.197
Weekends and source holidays
share the nonworking flag.

00:03:20.727 --> 00:03:22.317
That pooling is an assumption,

00:03:22.667 --> 00:03:25.977
and days sharing a key can still
have very different outcomes.

